WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.900
A mouse has no working rods or cones.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.400
Even so, it can tell a striped screen from a grey one.

3
00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:16.100
That is the finding in mice lacking rod and cone signalling.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.020
So which cells are doing the seeing?

5
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.600
One candidate is a small set of retinal ganglion cells.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.420
They carry their own light pigment, called melanopsin.

7
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:34.243
These cells are called intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells, or

8
00:00:34.243 --> 00:00:40.823
ipRGCs. An ipRGC makes its own light response with melanopsin.

9
00:00:41.443 --> 00:00:45.803
It also relays signals that rods and cones send through bipolar cells.

10
00:00:46.123 --> 00:00:47.663
So it has two jobs.

11
00:00:48.303 --> 00:00:50.903
Hold on to that. The rest of the film depends on it.

12
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:53.517
Now the first result.

13
00:00:54.537 --> 00:00:59.897
Mice lacking rod and cone signalling were able to discriminate a grating from an

14
00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:01.197
equally bright grey.

15
00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:07.617
Cells labelled with a melanopsin reporter heavily innervated a brain target, the

16
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:12.977
dLGN. So these cells give a route for coarse pattern information.

17
00:01:13.937 --> 00:01:15.517
It is not normal acuity.

18
00:01:16.377 --> 00:01:19.817
Those mice managed 0.16 cycles per degree.

19
00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:23.477
Wild type mice managed 0.55.

20
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.650
The second result comes from a different experiment.

21
00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:33.070
Mice lacking melanopsin showed deficits in contrast sensitivity.

22
00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:35.650
Both results come from mice.

23
00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:37.570
That sounds like the answer.

24
00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.150
But look at what was removed.

25
00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:43.550
Taking out the pigment removes one job.

26
00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.390
Taking out the cells removes both.

27
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.170
Now picture an ablation.

28
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.530
Remove the cells, and the animal does worse.

29
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:57.850
You have lost the melanopsin response and the rod and cone relay together.

30
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.270
The experiment cannot say which one mattered.

31
00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.070
Ablation implicates the cells.

32
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.150
It does not name the input.

33
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.790
A task can fail without the pigment mattering.

34
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:12.403
So how do you decide?

35
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:18.703
Compare melanopsin knockouts with animals whose ipRGCs are removed.

36
00:02:19.543 --> 00:02:25.143
Prefer an adult, timed manipulation, because loss during development can rewire

37
00:02:25.143 --> 00:02:27.703
targets. And remember the limits.

38
00:02:28.403 --> 00:02:33.443
These results come from mice, and grating discrimination is not normal acuity.

39
00:02:34.523 --> 00:02:38.323
Removing the cells and removing the pigment answer different questions.
